<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kilkenny Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> Castle is a castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nore">River Nore</a> in Kilkenny, Ireland, first built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> in the early 13th century on the site of an earlier Norman fortification. James Butler, 3rd Earl of Ormond, bought it in 1391, and it became the main seat of the Butler earls and dukes of Ormond. The family kept it until 1967, when it was handed to the people of Kilkenny.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilkenny-castle.jpg" alt="Kilkenny Castle" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tolivero · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilkenny-castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kilkenny Castle was built to control a fording point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nore">River Nore</a> and the junction of several routeways, and it was a symbol of Norman occupation. In its original 13th-century form it was a square castle with four large circular corner towers, three of which survive, and a massive ditch, part of which can still be seen on the Parade. The first castle, probably of wood, was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke</a>, known as Strongbow, possibly on the site of an earlier residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-giolla-phadraig">Mac Giolla Phádraig</a> kings of Osraighe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>'s daughter and heiress Isabel married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> in 1189, and in 1192 he appointed Geoffrey FitzRobert seneschal of Leinster, beginning a major phase of development at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>; excavations in 2019 uncovered the foundations of the great gatehouse Marshal built in the early 1200s. Lady Margaret Butler, the paternal grandmother of Anne Boleyn, was born in the castle. From 1642 to 1648 the Supreme Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a> met there, although its owner, James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormond, was the representative of Charles I. The east wall and north-east tower were damaged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s siege of 1650, and in 1661 the duke remodelled the castle as a château.</p><p>Run down by the 18th century, the castle was restored in the 19th towards its medieval appearance, and King Edward VII and Queen Alexandra visited it in 1904. The family sold its landed estates in 1903 under the Wyndham Land Act. In 1922, during the Irish Civil War, Free State forces besieged Republicans in the castle, doing a great deal of damage. In 1935 the family sold its contents for £6,000, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and left the castle empty for thirty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1967 Arthur Butler, 6th Marquess of Ormonde, sold the castle for £50 to the Castle Restoration Committee for the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, and the handover also marked the foundation of the Butler Society. The castle and its grounds are managed by the Office of Public Works, and the gardens and parkland are open to the public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kilkenny Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earls ruled their palatinate from <b>Kilkenny Castle</b>, bought in 1391, and Carrick-on-Suir, with their own courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ormond">Earldom of Ormond</a></li></ul></section>
